     8	QA the opencode coding agent itself. This skill maps each QA need to a tested
     9	helper script and a deep reference. Every script ships a `--self-test` that
    10	asserts its scenario against the live machine, so the scripts are both the QA
    11	tools and their own regression checks.
    12	
    13	Verified against opencode v1.17.7 (bun 1.3.12, macOS). Confirm the installed
    14	version with `opencode --version`; the surface is stable but always sanity
    15	check a flag with `opencode <cmd> --help`.
    16	
    17	## Golden rules (read before running anything)
    18	
    19	- READS of the live DB are safe and intended. Investigating sessions (Case D)
    20	  only reads `~/.local/share/opencode/opencode.db`.
    21	- Anything that SPAWNS opencode (serve, run, the TUI) must use an isolated XDG
    22	  sandbox so QA never writes junk sessions into the real DB. The bundled
    23	  scripts already do this; if you run opencode by hand for QA, set
    24	  `XDG_DATA_HOME` / `XDG_CONFIG_HOME` / `XDG_STATE_HOME` / `XDG_CACHE_HOME` to
    25	  temp dirs first.
    26	- Global text search over the `part` table is a multi-GB scan. Always scope it
    27	  (`--session`, `--recent`, or `--since`). The text script refuses an
    28	  unbounded scan on purpose.
    29	- The opencode source repo (`packages/opencode`) tests itself with `bun test`
    30	  and CANNOT run tests from the repo root. See `references/testing-harness.md`.
    31	
    32	## Setup
    33	
    34	Scripts live next to this file under `scripts/`. Invoke them from this skill
    35	directory (or with their absolute path):
    36	
    37	```bash
    38	cd <this-skill-dir>                        # .agents/skills/opencode-qa
    39	bash scripts/lib/common.sh --self-check    # confirm the harness + deps
    40	```
    41	
    42	**Docker is the default QA surface.** Run QA inside a disposable container that
    43	has the latest opencode and a copy of your config, with the host untouched:
    44	`script/agent/qa-docker.sh` (see [references/docker-qa.md](references/docker-qa.md)).
    45	The local scripts below are the fallback for when Docker is unavailable or on
    46	Windows.
    47	
    48	`common.sh` provides the shared harness (DB path, SQL escaping, isolated XDG
    49	sandbox, free port, server start/stop, and an EXIT-trap cleanup). It requires
    50	`opencode`, `sqlite3`, `curl`, `jq`, and `tmux` on PATH.
    51	
    52	## Router: pick your case
    53	
    54	| You want to... | Case | Script | Reference |
    55	|---|---|---|---|
    56	| Run opencode non-interactively / check a CLI command | A | `opencode run --format json` (inline) | `references/cli-commands.md` |
    57	| Find a session by its id | D | `scripts/db-session-by-id.sh <ses_id>` | `references/db-investigation.md` |
    58	| Find sessions by title/name | D | `scripts/db-session-by-name.sh "<text>"` | `references/db-investigation.md` |
    59	| Find sessions by message text | D | `scripts/db-session-by-text.sh --recent N "<text>"` | `references/db-investigation.md` |
    60	| Export a whole session as JSON | D | `scripts/export-roundtrip.sh <ses_id>` | `references/db-investigation.md` |
    61	| Check the HTTP server / an endpoint | B | `scripts/server-smoke.sh` | `references/server-api.md` |
    62	| Prove a hook / action / event fired | B | `scripts/sse-hook-probe.sh` | `references/events-hooks.md` |
    63	| Prove serve-topology wake runner-split (reproduced/fixed) | B | `scripts/serve-wake-split-probe.sh --expect reproduced\|fixed --evidence-dir DIR` (self-test: `--self-test`; fake LLM: `scripts/lib/fake-openai-server.mjs`) | `references/events-hooks.md` |
    64	| Smoke-test the TUI | C | `scripts/tui-smoke.sh` | `references/tui-tmux.md` |
    65	| Write/run a test in the opencode source | - | (bun test) | `references/testing-harness.md` |
    66	| Drive opencode from a Bun/TS script | - | (SDK) | `references/sdk.md` |
    67	
    68	## Case A: CLI / terminal works
    69	
    70	The canonical scriptable, non-interactive entry is `opencode run`. JSON mode
    71	emits one event per line so you can assert on it.
    72	
    73	```bash
    74	# stream structured events (types: text, tool_use, step_start, step_finish, reasoning, error)
    75	opencode run "list files in src" --format json
    76	# run a slash command
    77	opencode run --command commit
    78	# resume the last session
    79	opencode run -c "continue"
    80	# target an already-running server instead of booting one
    81	opencode run "explain auth" --attach http://127.0.0.1:4096 -p "$OPENCODE_SERVER_PASSWORD"
    82	```
    83	
    84	Other QA-useful commands: `opencode db path`, `opencode debug paths`,
    85	`opencode session list --format json`, `opencode models --verbose`. Full flag
    86	detail in `references/cli-commands.md`.
    87	
    88	## Case B: a specific hook, action, or event
    89	
    90	opencode publishes lifecycle events over Server-Sent Events at `GET /event`.
    91	Plugins observe the same events via the `event` hook, so seeing an event on the
    92	wire proves a hook would fire.
    93	
    94	```bash
    95	# prove the SSE plumbing works (isolated server, asserts server.connected)
    96	bash scripts/sse-hook-probe.sh --self-test
    97	
    98	# watch a REAL server for a specific event while you trigger an action
    99	bash scripts/sse-hook-probe.sh --attach http://127.0.0.1:4096 \
   100	  --password "$OPENCODE_SERVER_PASSWORD" --directory "$PWD" \
   101	  --event message.part.updated --timeout 30
   102	```
   103	
   104	Trigger an action over HTTP (fire-and-forget so the stream is not blocked):
   105	
   106	```bash
   107	curl -X POST -u opencode:$OPENCODE_SERVER_PASSWORD -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \
   108	  -d '{"parts":[{"type":"text","text":"say hi"}]}' \
   109	  "http://127.0.0.1:4096/session/<ses_id>/prompt_async?directory=$PWD"
   110	```
   111	
   112	A real prompt needs a configured provider, so run the watch-and-trigger pattern
   113	against your real server, not the isolated sandbox. Event-type catalog, the 21
   114	plugin hook points, and how to load a local plugin: `references/events-hooks.md`.
   115	Server start, auth, and routes: `references/server-api.md`.
   116	
   117	## Case C: the TUI
   118	
   119	```bash
   120	bash scripts/tui-smoke.sh --self-test
   121	```
   122	
   123	This launches the TUI under tmux in an isolated sandbox, confirms it renders
   124	(`capture-pane`), confirms `send-keys` reaches the composer, tears the tmux
   125	session down, and verifies the real DB session count is unchanged.
   126	
   127	Honest verdict: tmux is fine for SMOKE (did it boot, render, accept a key) but
   128	fragile for asserting conversation output (the TUI is a 60fps full-screen app).
   129	For real behavior assertions use Case A (`opencode run`), Case B (server API +
   130	SSE), or the TUI control HTTP API (`POST /tui/append-prompt`,
   131	`POST /tui/submit-prompt`, `POST /tui/execute-command`). Details and the manual
   132	tmux recipe: `references/tui-tmux.md`.
   133	
   134	## Case D: investigate sessions in the DB
   135	
   136	Read-only against the live SQLite DB. The `session` table is small (title and
   137	id lookups are instant); message text lives in the multi-GB `part` table, so
   138	text search must be scoped.
   139	
   140	```bash
   141	# by id
   142	bash scripts/db-session-by-id.sh ses_3a4ee6335ffedFB8f76BPU1Eb3
   143	# by title / name (newest first; second arg = limit)
   144	bash scripts/db-session-by-name.sh "auth refactor" 20
   145	# by message text - scope with --session, --recent N, or --since "<window>"
   146	bash scripts/db-session-by-text.sh --session ses_3a4e... "ULTRAWORK"
   147	bash scripts/db-session-by-text.sh --recent 50 "permission denied"
   148	bash scripts/db-session-by-text.sh --since "7 days" --limit 50 "TODO"
   149	# export an entire session as clean JSON
   150	bash scripts/export-roundtrip.sh ses_3a4e... > session.json
   151	```
   152	
   153	Ad hoc queries: `opencode db "<SQL>" --format json`. Schema, tested query
   154	shapes with timings, the legacy `message`/`part` vs V2 `session_message`
   155	distinction, and the 25 GB caveat: `references/db-investigation.md`.
   156	
   157	## Scripts index
   158	
   159	Run any script with `--self-test` to verify it against the live machine, or
   160	`-h` for usage. DB-read scripts are read-only; serve/sse/tui scripts use an
   161	isolated sandbox and clean up on exit.
   162	
   163	| Script | Case | Self-test asserts |
   164	|---|---|---|
   165	| `scripts/lib/common.sh --self-check` | - | deps present, DB path resolves, SQL escaping, free port, sandbox auto-removed |
   166	| `scripts/db-session-by-id.sh` | D | id round-trips for a real session |
   167	| `scripts/db-session-by-name.sh` | D | a derived title needle returns >=1 row |
   168	| `scripts/db-session-by-text.sh` | D | scoped search hits; unbounded scan refused; bounded search <30s |
   169	| `scripts/export-roundtrip.sh` | D | export stdout is valid JSON and `.info.id` round-trips |
   170	| `scripts/server-smoke.sh` | B | `/global/health` healthy, `/doc` >=100 paths, no-auth -> 401 |
   171	| `scripts/sse-hook-probe.sh` | B | `/event` opens and delivers `server.connected` |
   172	| `scripts/tui-smoke.sh` | C | TUI renders under tmux, tears down, real DB untouched |
   173	
   174	## Risks and caveats
   175	
   176	- 25 GB part table: never run an unbounded text scan. Use `--session`,
   177	  `--recent`, or `--since`. A naive `JOIN ... WHERE session.time_created >= X`
   178	  scans oldest-first and can take ~50s; the scripts use an `IN`-subquery on the
   179	  newest sessions (~20ms).
   180	- `opencode export` writes its banner to STDERR; pipe with `2>/dev/null` before
   181	  `jq` or you will get a parse error.
   182	- The server enforces auth only when `OPENCODE_SERVER_PASSWORD` is set;
   183	  otherwise it runs unsecured. Authenticated calls use `-u opencode:$PASS`.
   184	  Unauthenticated calls to a secured server return HTTP 401.
   185	- Installed binary vs dev source: cite dev source paths for internals but
   186	  verify flags against the installed `opencode <cmd> --help`.
   187	- Isolation: any QA that spawns opencode must use an isolated XDG sandbox so it
   188	  never pollutes the real DB. Prove it by comparing
   189	  `sqlite3 "$(opencode db path)" "SELECT count(*) FROM session"` before and
   190	  after.
   191	- TUI output assertions are fragile; use the API for real assertions.
   192	
   193	## References
   194	
   195	- `references/cli-commands.md` - every QA-relevant opencode subcommand and flag
   196	- `references/db-investigation.md` - DB schema, tested queries, the 25 GB caveat
   197	- `references/server-api.md` - server start, auth, route catalog, /doc
   198	- `references/events-hooks.md` - SSE endpoints, event types, plugin hooks
   199	- `references/tui-tmux.md` - tmux recipe, isolation, TUI control API
   200	- `references/testing-harness.md` - how opencode tests itself (bun test)
   201	- `references/sdk.md` - the @opencode-ai/sdk client (reference only)
   202	- `references/docker-qa.md` - run QA in a disposable Docker container (default; local is the fallback)

